  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nThe omega block pattern will continue to provide periodic shower \nactivity with scattered thunderstorms at times. The strong low-\npressure system over the west coast has been able push multiple mid-\nlevel vort maxima across the Central CONUS. For today, this pushed \nthe mid-level ridge axis northeastward and promoted localized mid-\nlevel height falls across the Plains and lower Missouri River \nValley. Surface pressure falls have also picked up in pace this \nafternoon, providing better low-level convergence this afternoon \nthan was present yesterday. While there has been lift and plenty of \nmoisture, mid and upper-level flow remain fairly week, limiting wind \nshear and thus preventing any strong convection from fully \norganizing. A slight northward trend has been noted during the later \nafternoon hours of Thursday. This pattern will continue to persist \nthrough Friday, with multiple waves of showers moving through, and \neach one progressing slightly northeastward as more vort maxima \neject from the western CONUS troughing pattern. Periods of mid-level \nheight rises will remain possible while we are in this omega block \npattern, which localized subsidence would clear skies between rounds \nof showers, and allow for boundary layer destabilization. This \nshould foster the development of scattered thunderstorms Friday \nwithin a broad area of shower activity, but deep layer shear fails \nto materialize which limits any severe threat or ability for \nactivity to organize. Persistent low-level flow from the south will \ncontinue to provide moisture transport, along with WAA that should \nhelp to maintain air temperatures in the lower mid 80s. \n\nA stronger short-wave trough will eject from the main disturbance \nparked over the western CONUS Saturday into Sunday, which should \nalso provide enough dCVA into the Plains and lower Missouri River \nValley to develop a surface cyclone, increasing the overall forcing. \nMoisture transport will continue in this regime, leading to \ncontinued shower and scattered storm activity across much of the \nforecast area. While mid-level flow may start to increase, the \nbetter forecast shear environment remains over the Central Plains, \nand therefore keeps the low-end severe probabilities over the \nweekend west of the forecast area. Most of the ensembles maintain \nsome probabilities for measurable rainfall through Tuesday of this \nweek, before this blocking pattern is disrupted and is able to \nbecome progressive. However as with most blocking patterns, the \nfinal breakup can be hard resolve, which leads to increased \nuncertainty for both precipitation and temperatures trends through \nthe middle of next week. At some point though, the series of vort \nmaxima should shift northward, with a few days of subsidence that \nwill eventually dry conditions out across the area.",
